/// The value range syntax builds: `a..b` is `Range(start: a, end: b,
/// inclusive: false)` and `a..=b` is `Range(start: a, end: b, inclusive: true)`.
///
/// A range is an ordinary struct. Its fields are read and written like any
/// struct's, two ranges are equal when their fields are, and its hash is
/// derived from the same fields, so a range can be a set element or a map key.
/// A `for` loop steps through a range of any `Steppable` type from `start`
/// up to `end`: the integers, one-character strings by code point, and a
/// class through its own `successor`. The `in` operator tests a value against
/// a range of any type `<` orders:
/// `x in r` holds when `start <= x` and `x < end`, or `x <= end` when
/// `inclusive`.
public struct Range<T>
    start T
    end T
    inclusive bool
